    {
      "heading": "Introduction: The Clan System of the Murle People",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The Murle people of South Sudan have a social organization built on strong connections between ancestry, family, land, leadership, and tradition.",
        "At the foundation of this organization is the clan system, known among the Murle as Kidoŋwa.",
        "A clan is not simply a family name. It is a cultural institution that connects people to their ancestors, defines relationships between families, and carries responsibilities that have been passed through generations.",
        "Through clans, Murle communities maintain identity, traditional authority, and a connection between the past and present."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Meaning of Kidoŋwa",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Kidoŋwa represents a wider ancestral group connected through lineage and shared history.",
        "A person's clan identity is inherited through family connections and remains an important part of who they are within Murle society.",
        "The clan system helps people understand their relationships with others, their cultural responsibilities, and their place within the community."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Importance of Clans in Murle Society",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Traditional Murle society depended on cooperation between different clans.",
        "Clans provided a structure for leadership, social organization, cultural practices, and community responsibilities.",
        "They helped maintain unity among people living across different areas of Murle land, including Pibor, Boma, Likuangole, Gumuruk, Kengen, and surrounding communities."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Clans and Ancestral Identity",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Ancestry is one of the strongest foundations of Murle identity.",
